questions.answer:
const LOG = /^(?<date>\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}) (?<level>\w+) (?<message>.+)$/;

const parseLogs = (lines) =>
  lines.map((line) => {
    const match = LOG.exec(line);
    return match ? { ...match.groups } : null;
  });

console.log(parseLogs(lines));
// [
//   { date: '2026-03-01', level: 'INFO', message: 'Kakade logged in' },
//   { date: '2026-03-02', level: 'WARN', message: 'Asha session expired' },
//   null
// ]

Use ^...$ so a partial line does not pass. Named groups keep the destructuring readable and avoid counting parentheses. groups is a null-prototype object; spreading it returns a normal object that prints and serializes predictably. exec with a non-global regex is correct here because you test one line at a time; a global LOG would advance lastIndex between lines and skip matches. If you later add the g flag, use matchAll over the whole text instead of reusing exec. message uses .+ so it can contain spaces; it stops at the end because of $.

