const text = "Contact kakade@dev.io or asha@example.com today";

const maskEmails = (text) => {
  // your code
};

console.log(maskEmails(text));

questions.answer:
const maskEmails = (text) =>
  text.replace(/(?<user>[\w.+-]+)@(?<domain>[\w.-]+\.[a-z]{2,})/gi, (match, user, domain) =>
    `${user[0]}***@${domain}`
  );

console.log(maskEmails(text));
// Contact k***@dev.io or a***@example.com today

The replace callback receives (match, p1, p2, offset, input, groups) for numbered groups. Here the first two parameters after the match are user and domain, so user[0] is safe as long as the group is non-empty. Return the replacement string; returning the original match would leave the email unchanged. The g flag is required to replace every email, and i makes the domain case-insensitive. This is masking for display or logs, not a validation pattern: a stricter email regex would be longer and still would not prove the address exists. Never build HTML from the result without escaping.

